---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Base
  title="Design Org AI Initiatives | Jeremy Hoover"
  description="How I identified gaps in org-wide AI adoption, created foundational capability, established governance for a shared knowledge system, and trained designers across the organization."
>
  <article class="section case-study case-study--orange">
    <header class="case-hero">
      <div class="container container--narrow">
        <a href={`${base}/#projects`} class="back"><span class="back-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Back to selected projects</span></a>

        <span class="tag"><span>Org-wide influence</span><span>Design organization</span><span>AI adoption</span></span>
        <h1>Building the conditions for AI adoption across the design org</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          I didn't wait for the org to have an AI strategy. Three times I identified what was
          missing, brought it to leadership, drove it from proof of concept to required workflow,
          and trained the people using it. The work spans a content-design agent, a governed
          knowledge system, and org-wide training that is now part of how the organization works.
        </p>

        <div class="outcomes-strip" aria-label="Project outcomes">
          <div class="outcome">
            <span class="outcome__num">Hundreds</span>
            <span class="outcome__label">Designers across the design org using an agent I helped create</span>
          </div>
          <div class="outcome">
            <span class="outcome__num">Three</span>
            <span class="outcome__label">Initiatives from initial idea to required org workflow</span>
          </div>
          <div class="outcome">
            <span class="outcome__num">Governed</span>
            <span class="outcome__label">One contribution, review, and quality model for the shared knowledge base</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="case-body">
      <div class="container case-shell">
        <aside class="disclosure" aria-label="Project disclosure">
          <strong>About the evidence</strong>
          <p>This project was completed inside Microsoft. I reconstructed the diagrams with generic labels and removed internal details that are not public.</p>
        </aside>

        <section class="case-summary" aria-labelledby="summary-title">
          <div class="reading-column">
            <h2 id="summary-title">At a glance</h2>
            <dl class="case-meta">
              <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Senior Content Designer; initiative lead, KB governance owner, and org-level trainer</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Scope</dt><dd>Design organization spanning multiple teams and geographies</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Pattern</dt><dd>Gap identification, leadership engagement, v-team, POC, GA, required workflow</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Status</dt><dd>All three initiatives active and required</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Collaboration</dt><dd>Senior org leaders, principal design managers, DesignOps, repository owners, reviewers, and subject-matter experts</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Evidence</dt><dd>Verified internal adoption, VP-level recognition, and internal showcase presentation</dd></div>
            </dl>
          </div>
        </section>

        <nav class="case-toc" aria-label="Design org AI initiatives sections">
          <span class="case-toc__label">Jump to a section</span>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="#gap">The gap</a></li>
            <li><a href="#agent">Writing agent</a></li>
            <li><a href="#kb">Knowledge base</a></li>
            <li><a href="#training">Training</a></li>
            <li><a href="#required">What required means</a></li>
            <li><a href="#reflection">Reflection</a></li>
          </ul>
        </nav>

        <section id="gap" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>The org-level gap</h2>
          <p>Three things were true at the same time. Designers lacked a shared AI capability they could trust and build from. The org's collective knowledge was scattered across files and wikis and not being maintained. And there was no systematic way to build the skills people needed to use either one.</p>
          <p>Solving one of those problems would have helped. Solving all three created something different: the conditions for AI adoption to take hold and become part of how the org works.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="agent" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>Content-design writing agent</h2>
          <p>Before this work, the material content designers needed was scattered across Figma files, wikis, and email threads. Getting a consistent, useful response from an AI assistant required rebuilding context from scratch every time.</p>
          <p>I saw a better way to package it. I created the foundational files that gave the agent structure: the content, the organization, and the connections between things that had never been connected before. I was among the first in the organization, and possibly the company, to approach content-design capability this way.</p>
          <p>I presented the work at an internal design org showcase. Others built on what I created, and the agent grew. It now serves hundreds of designers across the design org, with additional use from product managers working on content design and accessibility.</p>
        </section>

        <figure class="evidence-figure" aria-labelledby="agent-caption">
          <div class="before-after" role="img" aria-label="Before and after showing fragmented content sources replaced by structured agent capability">
            <section class="ba-panel ba-panel--before">
              <span class="ba-label">Before</span>
              <h3>Scattered content</h3>
              <ul>
                <li>Figma files</li>
                <li>Wikis</li>
                <li>Email threads</li>
                <li>Rebuilt from scratch each time</li>
              </ul>
            </section>
            <div class="ba-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
            <section class="ba-panel ba-panel--after">
              <span class="ba-label">After</span>
              <h3>Structured agent capability</h3>
              <ul>
                <li>Foundational files</li>
                <li>Connected content</li>
                <li>Reusable by hundreds</li>
                <li>Extended by the org</li>
              </ul>
            </section>
          </div>
          <figcaption id="agent-caption">
            <strong>From scattered to structured.</strong> Foundational files connected content that had never been brought together, giving the agent a reusable capability the org could build from.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <section id="kb" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>Knowledge base</h2>
          <p>The knowledge base had stalled. Years of collective design knowledge sat in a system that was neither growing nor being maintained.</p>
          <p>I identified the gap and brought it to a senior design org leader. He connected me with the design manager overseeing the effort. From there, I worked with that manager and a small v-team to define the approach, build the proof of concept, and drive it up the org. The concept was well received at VP level and became a full initiative.</p>
          <p>The KB is now a required part of how every designer in the org works. Everyone is expected to contribute to it. After helping drive the proof of concept to general availability, I stepped up to lead the governance plan for how the organization submits, reviews, improves, merges, and closes contributions and gap logs.</p>
          <p>I designed the end-to-end operating model, including entry types and templates, a review rubric, contributor and reviewer workflows, reviewer triage, merge responsibilities, and a process for bringing subject-matter experts into unresolved knowledge gaps. I also created repository-based skills and prompts that guide people through the work at the point of need.</p>
          <p>I piloted the process with a small reviewer group, gathered feedback, and refined both the model and the repository before expanding it. I trained reviewers on the standard and partnered with principal design managers, DesignOps, and owners of the broader design repository, where the KB is a core part of the organization's AI strategy.</p>
          <p>The work moved my role from helping launch the KB to owning how its quality and contribution system operates. I continue to run and refine the process as the organization contributes, reviewers encounter new cases, and the knowledge base evolves.</p>
        </section>

        <figure class="evidence-figure" aria-labelledby="kb-caption">
          <ol class="kb-stages kb-stages--governance">
            <li>
              <span class="stage-num">01</span>
              <strong>Submit</strong>
              <small>Use a defined entry type and template</small>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="stage-num">02</span>
              <strong>Triage</strong>
              <small>Route contributions and gap logs to reviewers</small>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="stage-num">03</span>
              <strong>Review</strong>
              <small>Apply the shared quality rubric</small>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="stage-num">04</span>
              <strong>Improve</strong>
              <small>Resolve feedback and partner with SMEs</small>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="stage-num">05</span>
              <strong>Merge or close</strong>
              <small>Publish trusted knowledge or resolve the log</small>
            </li>
          </ol>
          <figcaption id="kb-caption">
            <strong>A contribution system for the entire design org.</strong> Templates, triage, a review rubric, SME partnership, and clear merge ownership turn required contributions into trusted, maintainable knowledge.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <section class="reading-column content-section" aria-labelledby="enablement-title">
          <h2 id="enablement-title">Designing support into the workflow</h2>
          <p>A governance model only works if people can follow it. We considered office hours, standing support sessions, and a library of training videos. Those options could help, but they also separated guidance from the moment someone was contributing or reviewing.</p>
          <p>I recommended putting reusable skills, workflows, and prompts directly in the repository, then supporting them through the existing org channel. Contributors can ask questions where the broader community already works or set up time for a specific issue, while the repository provides consistent guidance every time.</p>
          <p>This approach makes the process available across the organization without depending on a recurring meeting or one person repeating the same instructions. It also gives us a practical way to update the guidance as the governance model matures.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="reading-column content-section" aria-labelledby="platform-title">
          <h2 id="platform-title">The broader org platform and the AI design repository</h2>
          <p>The content-design agent and the knowledge base are part of a larger org-wide platform that multiple teams have contributed to. Together they give the design org a shared foundation: reusable content capability, structured knowledge, and context for the work the org actually does.</p>
          <p>The AI design repository I built for my business group connects to this platform. It draws on the KB, the content-design agent, and other org capabilities rather than rebuilding them. That relationship matters because it shows how org-level infrastructure becomes useful at the team level.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="training" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>Training across the org</h2>
          <p>Building something and getting people to use it are different problems. I focused on both.</p>
          <p>I am a key trainer for AI adoption across my design org. I cover content design, the knowledge base, accessibility, context gathering, and research. I teach design managers and individual contributors. The goal is not to explain tools. It is to help designers understand when and how to use AI responsibly in the work they already do.</p>
          <p>Some of that training happens in formal sessions. Some of it happens in the demonstrations I build for senior leadership visits. When VPs came to see what the design team was doing with AI, I built those demos. That kind of trust is earned through consistently delivering work that is ready to represent the org.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="required" class="content-section">
          <div class="reading-column">
            <h2>What required means</h2>
            <p>Each of the three initiatives is now a required part of how the org works. This shift did not happen because leadership mandated tools. It happened because the tools were useful, the knowledge system was maintained, and people knew how to work with both. I was a driving force in all three.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="conditions-grid" aria-label="Adoption conditions">
            <div>
              <strong>Content-design agent</strong>
              <span>Foundational capability the org builds from</span>
            </div>
            <div>
              <strong>Knowledge base</strong>
              <span>Required contribution from every designer</span>
            </div>
            <div>
              <strong>Training</strong>
              <span>Responsible AI practice across design managers and ICs</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="reflection" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>
          <p>I think the most valuable thing I built was not the product. It was the practice. The content-design agent, the knowledge base, and the training are all useful. But what made them stick was the pattern: identify the gap, earn the support, drive the work, teach the result.</p>
          <p>This kind of work is often invisible in a job title. It shows up in what the org is doing, not in what any one person owns. I think that is what org-level contribution actually looks like.</p>
        </section>

        <aside class="pattern-cta" aria-labelledby="pattern-title">
          <div>
            <span class="tag">Active and required</span>
            <h2 id="pattern-title">A pattern, not a project</h2>
            <p>All three initiatives remain active. The content-design agent is part of the org platform. The knowledge base is a required workflow. The training continues as the tools and practices evolve.</p>
          </div>
        </aside>

        <hr class="divider" />
        <nav class="case-pagination" aria-label="Case study navigation">
          <a href={`${base}/work/hackathon`} class="case-pagination__link">
            <span class="case-pagination__icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="case-pagination__content"><span class="case-pagination__direction">Previous project</span><span class="case-pagination__title">Hackathon PM agent</span></span>
          </a>
          <a href={`${base}/work/fabric-cli`} class="case-pagination__link case-pagination__link--next">
            <span class="case-pagination__content"><span class="case-pagination__direction">Next project</span><span class="case-pagination__title">Fabric CLI AI skills</span></span>
            <span class="case-pagination__icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .case-study { padding-top: 0; }
  .tag { display: inline-block; margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  h1 { margin-block: var(--space-4) var(--space-6); }
  .lede { max-width: 58ch; margin-bottom: var(--space-12); font-size: var(--size-xl); line-height: 1.6; }
  .outcome { display: grid; grid-template-rows: 2.25rem auto; align-content: start; gap: var(--space-3); transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .outcome:hover { transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .case-study .outcome__num { align-self: start; font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 11cqw, 1.875rem); line-height: 1.15; white-space: nowrap; }
  .case-study .outcome__label { max-width: 25ch; font-size: var(--size-sm); line-height: 1.55; color: var(--muted); }
  .case-shell { max-width: calc(var(--narrow) + 6rem); }
  .reading-column { width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); margin-inline: auto; }
  .content-section { scroll-margin-top: 90px; }
  .content-section h2, .case-summary h2 { margin-bottom: var(--space-6); }
  .content-section:not(:first-child) h2 { margin-top: var(--space-16); }
  .content-section p { margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  .content-section ul { margin: var(--space-4) 0 var(--space-6) var(--space-6); }
  .content-section li { margin-bottom: var(--space-2); }

  .disclosure { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 150px 1fr; gap: var(--space-6); width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); margin: 0 auto var(--space-12); padding: var(--space-6); border: 1px solid var(--portfolio-blue-soft); border-left: 4px solid var(--portfolio-blue); border-radius: 12px; background: #fff; }
  .disclosure strong { color: var(--portfolio-blue); font-size: 0.75rem; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .disclosure p { color: var(--muted); font-size: var(--size-sm); }

  .case-meta { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--space-3); }
  .case-meta div { padding: var(--space-6); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-left: 4px solid var(--portfolio-blue-soft); border-radius: 12px; background: #fff; transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .case-meta div:hover { transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .case-meta dt { margin-bottom: var(--space-2); color: var(--portfolio-blue); font-size: 0.75rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .case-meta dd { color: var(--portfolio-ink); font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.5; }

  .case-toc { max-width: var(--narrow); margin: var(--space-8) auto var(--space-16); padding-block: var(--space-6); border-block: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .case-toc__label { display: block; margin-bottom: var(--space-3); color: var(--portfolio-blue); font-size: 0.6875rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: 0.1em; line-height: 1.2; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .case-toc ul { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.5rem 0; margin: 0; list-style: none; }
  .case-toc li { --toc-divider: var(--portfolio-coral); display: flex; align-items: center; }
  .case-toc li + li { margin-left: var(--space-4); }
  .case-toc li + li::before { content: ''; width: 2px; height: 16px; margin-right: var(--space-4); border-radius: 999px; background: var(--toc-divider); }
  .case-toc li:nth-child(3n) { --toc-divider: var(--portfolio-yellow); }
  .case-toc li:nth-child(3n + 1) { --toc-divider: var(--portfolio-blue); }
  .case-toc a { display: inline-flex; min-height: 32px; align-items: center; color: var(--portfolio-ink); font-size: 0.875rem; font-weight: 650; line-height: 1.3; text-decoration: none; }
  .case-toc a:hover { color: var(--portfolio-blue); text-decoration: underline; text-decoration-color: var(--portfolio-coral); text-decoration-thickness: 2px; text-underline-offset: 5px; }

  .evidence-figure { width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); margin: var(--space-12) auto var(--space-16); }
  .evidence-figure figcaption { width: 100%; margin-top: var(--space-4); color: var(--muted); font-size: var(--size-sm); }
  .evidence-figure figcaption strong { color: var(--portfolio-ink); }

  .before-after { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0,1fr) auto minmax(0,1fr); gap: var(--space-3); align-items: stretch; padding: var(--space-4); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 20px; background: #fff; }
  .ba-panel { padding: var(--space-6); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 14px; transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .ba-panel--before { background: var(--portfolio-cream); }
  .ba-panel--after { background: var(--portfolio-blue-soft); }
  .ba-panel:hover { transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .ba-label { display: block; color: var(--portfolio-coral); font-size: 0.7rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: var(--space-2); }
  .ba-panel h3 { margin-bottom: var(--space-4); color: var(--portfolio-blue); }
  .ba-panel ul { margin-left: var(--space-4); }
  .ba-panel li { margin-bottom: var(--space-2); color: var(--muted); font-size: var(--size-sm); }
  .ba-arrow { display: grid; place-items: center; color: var(--portfolio-blue); font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 800; }

  .kb-stages { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(5, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--space-2); margin: 0; padding: var(--space-4); border: 1px solid #b8c9ee; border-radius: 20px; background: var(--portfolio-blue-soft); list-style: none; }
  .kb-stages li { position: relative; display: flex; min-height: 185px; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-2); padding: var(--space-4); border-radius: 12px; background: #fff; transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .kb-stages li:hover { z-index: 2; transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .kb-stages li::after { content: '→'; position: absolute; top: 50%; right: -0.8rem; z-index: 1; color: var(--portfolio-blue); font-size: 1.2rem; font-weight: 800; transform: translateY(-50%); }
  .kb-stages li:last-child::after { content: none; }
  .stage-num { display: grid; width: 1.75rem; height: 1.75rem; place-items: center; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--portfolio-blue); color: #fff; font-size: 0.7rem; font-weight: 800; }
  .kb-stages strong { color: var(--portfolio-ink); font-size: 0.875rem; line-height: 1.35; }
  .kb-stages small { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.78rem; line-height: 1.45; }

  .conditions-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); gap: var(--space-3); margin: var(--space-8) auto var(--space-4); }
  .conditions-grid div { display: flex; min-height: 140px; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-3); padding: var(--space-6); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 14px; background: #fff; transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .conditions-grid div:hover { transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .conditions-grid strong { color: var(--portfolio-blue); line-height: 1.35; }
  .conditions-grid span { color: var(--muted); font-size: var(--size-sm); line-height: 1.5; }

  .pattern-cta { width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); margin: var(--space-16) auto 0; padding: clamp(1.5rem, 5vw, 3rem); border-radius: 20px; background: var(--portfolio-blue); }
  .pattern-cta .tag { color: var(--portfolio-yellow); }
  .pattern-cta h2 { margin-bottom: var(--space-3); color: #fff; }
  .pattern-cta p { color: rgba(255,255,255,0.82); }

  .divider { margin-block: var(--space-16) var(--space-8); }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .case-toc ul { gap: 0.5rem 1.5rem; }
    .case-toc li + li { margin-left: 0; }
    .case-toc li + li::before { content: none; }
    .kb-stages { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
    .kb-stages li { min-height: 150px; }
    .kb-stages li::after { content: none; }
    .conditions-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .conditions-grid div { min-height: 0; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .content-section { scroll-margin-top: 130px; }
    .disclosure { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--space-2); padding: var(--space-4); }
    .case-meta { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .case-meta div { padding: var(--space-4); }
    .case-toc { margin-top: var(--space-6); margin-bottom: var(--space-12); padding-block: var(--space-4); }
    .case-toc ul { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); width: 100%; gap: var(--space-1) var(--space-6); }
    .case-toc a { min-height: 44px; }
    .before-after { grid-template-columns: 1fr; padding: var(--space-3); }
    .ba-arrow { transform: rotate(90deg); }
    .kb-stages { grid-template-columns: 1fr; padding: var(--space-3); }
    .kb-stages li { min-height: auto; }
    .pattern-cta { padding: var(--space-8); }
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .outcome:hover, .case-meta div:hover, .ba-panel:hover,
    .kb-stages li:hover, .conditions-grid div:hover { transform: none; }
  }
</style>
